Here is the table version of the two-state DP. For every day, `dp[day][0]` stores the best profit when holding no stock, and `dp[day][1]` stores the best profit while holding one. Selling moves from state `1` to state `0` and pays the fee.

For the sample prices `[1, 3, 2, 8, 4, 9]`, the no-stock value reaches `5` after selling at `8`, then reaches `8` after the final sale at `9`.

```java
class Solution {
    public int stockBuySell(int[] arr, int n, int fee) {
        if (n < 2) {
            return 0;
        }

        int[][] dp = new int[n][2];
        dp[0][0] = 0;
        dp[0][1] = -arr[0];

        for (int day = 1; day < n; day++) {
            dp[day][0] = Math.max(
                dp[day - 1][0],
                dp[day - 1][1] + arr[day] - fee
            );

            dp[day][1] = Math.max(
                dp[day - 1][1],
                dp[day - 1][0] - arr[day]
            );
        }

        return dp[n - 1][0];
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O(n)`.
Space complexity: `O(n)` for the DP table.

The fee can be included in the buying price. I keep the cheapest effective price seen so far. When the current price is more than that value, a sale is worthwhile. After adding the profit, setting the effective price to `price - fee` lets the next trade start from the current sale.

This avoids a DP table, but the greedy update is less obvious than the two-state method. It does not modify `arr`.

```cpp
class Solution{
public:
    int stockBuySell(vector<int> arr, int n, int fee){
        if (n < 2) {
            return 0;
        }

        int profit = 0;
        int effectiveBuy = arr[0];

        for (int i = 1; i < n; i++) {
            if (arr[i] < effectiveBuy) {
                effectiveBuy = arr[i];
            } else if (arr[i] > effectiveBuy + fee) {
                profit += arr[i] - effectiveBuy - fee;
                effectiveBuy = arr[i] - fee;
            }
        }

        return profit;
    }
};
```

Time complexity: `O(n)`.
Space complexity: `O(1)`.

I kept two values while scanning the prices. `cash` is the best profit when no stock is held, and `hold` is the best result when one stock is held. Selling subtracts the fee once. A saved `oldCash` makes sure the buy decision uses the previous day's value.

For `[1, 3, 2, 8, 4, 9]` with fee `2`, the profitable trades are `1 -> 8` for `5` and `4 -> 9` for `3`, giving `8`.

```cpp
class Solution{
public:
    int stockBuySell(vector<int> arr, int n, int fee){
        if (n < 2) {
            return 0;
        }

        int cash = 0;
        int hold = -arr[0];

        for (int i = 1; i < n; i++) {
            int oldCash = cash;
            int oldHold = hold;

            cash = max(oldCash, oldHold + arr[i] - fee);
            hold = max(oldHold, oldCash - arr[i]);
        }

        return cash;
    }
};
```

Time complexity: `O(n)`.
Space complexity: `O(1)`.

This version works from the last day toward the first. `canBuy` means there is no stock in hand, while `holding` means a stock is already owned. Each state only needs the values from the next day, so two pairs of integers are enough.

The input array is only read and is not changed. With an empty or one-day list, no complete transaction can be made.

```java
class Solution {
    public int stockBuySell(int[] arr, int n, int fee) {
        if (n < 2) {
            return 0;
        }

        int nextCanBuy = 0;
        int nextHolding = 0;

        for (int day = n - 1; day >= 0; day--) {
            int currentCanBuy = Math.max(
                -arr[day] + nextHolding,
                nextCanBuy
            );

            int currentHolding = Math.max(
                arr[day] - fee + nextCanBuy,
                nextHolding
            );

            nextCanBuy = currentCanBuy;
            nextHolding = currentHolding;
        }

        return nextCanBuy;
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O(n)`.
Extra space: `O(1)`.

A recursive state is described by the day and whether a stock is currently held. On a buy day, the choices are buying or skipping. On a holding day, the choices are selling or waiting. Results are saved in a table because the same state can be reached more than once.

Dry run for prices `[1, 3, 2, 8, 4, 9]` and fee `2`: the recursion can choose buy at `1`, sell at `8`, then buy at `4` and sell at `9`. The stored states combine these choices into profit `8`.

```python
class Solution:
    def stockBuySell(self, arr, n, fee):
        if n < 2:
            return 0

        memo = {}

        def best(day, holding):
            if day == n:
                return 0

            state = (day, holding)
            if state in memo:
                return memo[state]

            skip = best(day + 1, holding)

            if holding:
                action = arr[day] - fee + best(day + 1, False)
            else:
                action = -arr[day] + best(day + 1, True)

            memo[state] = max(skip, action)
            return memo[state]

        return best(0, False)
```

Time complexity: `O(n)` because there are two states per day.
Space complexity: `O(n)` for the memo table and recursion stack.

## Solution
```java[]
class Solution {
    public int stockBuySell(int[] arr, int n, int fee) {

        int[] next = new int[2]; 
        int[] cur = new int[2];

        for (int i = n - 1; i >= 0; i--) {
            // canBuy = 1
            cur[1] = Math.max(-arr[i] + next[0], next[1]);

            // canBuy = 0
            cur[0] = Math.max(arr[i] + next[1] - fee, next[0]);

            next[0] = cur[0];
            next[1] = cur[1];
        }

        return next[1];
    }
}

```

